(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-hf-digoxin-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Digoxin Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is digoxin an add-on rather than a mandatory drug in heart failure?","choices":["It shortens survival","It gives no survival benefit","It does not relieve symptoms","It cannot be given with diuretics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mandatory drugs are the ones that prolong life; digoxin only relieves symptoms and reduces hospitalizations, so it is optional. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which effect describes the older, inotropic understanding of how digoxin works?","choices":["Lower preload through increased urine flow","Greater force of cardiac muscle contraction","Blocked pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node","Balanced dilation of arteries and veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The older view is a positive inotropic action, a stronger contraction; the newer view adds neurohormonal effects. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which inotropic drug lowers heart rate instead of raising it?","choices":["Dobutamine","Milrinone","Dopamine","Digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Resetting the baroreflex and raising parasympathetic activity slow the heart rate, while the contraction becomes stronger. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which measurement is followed against a target range during digoxin therapy?","choices":["Plasma renin activity","Serum creatine kinase","Platelet count","Serum digoxin concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A serum digoxin concentration is followed against a target range, and higher concentrations may be linked to worse outcomes in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which combination of electrolyte disturbances raises the risk of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Low potassium, low magnesium and high calcium","Low potassium, high magnesium and low calcium","High sodium, high magnesium and low calcium","Low potassium, high magnesium and high calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia and hypercalcemia all make digoxin toxicity more likely and are cautions for its use. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"In which patient with heart failure is digoxin mainly added?","choices":["One newly diagnosed and without symptoms","One still symptomatic on optimal standard drugs","One in whom survival benefit is the only goal","One whose pulse is already very slow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin is mainly added for patients who remain symptomatic despite optimal doses of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta blockers and diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In which part of the heart does digoxin raise parasympathetic activity?","choices":["Ventricular contractile muscle","Coronary artery walls","Atrioventricular node","Pulmonary vein sleeves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic activity rises in the atrioventricular node and conduction system, which slows conduction and heart rate. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A patient with heart failure reports seeing yellow-green halos around streetlights. Which drug is the likely cause?","choices":["Carvedilol","Spironolactone","Digoxin","Furosemide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Yellow-green halos and blurred vision are visual signs of digoxin toxicity, which calls for checking a serum concentration. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which statement about digoxin and the course of heart failure is correct?","choices":["It slows progression as angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors do","It halts progression when added to a diuretic","It slows progression at higher concentrations","It has no evidence of slowing progression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. There is no evidence that digoxin slows disease progression; it improves symptoms but is not disease-modifying. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which ventricular rhythm problem contraindicates digoxin?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Normal sinus rhythm","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Premature ventricular beats and ventricular tachycardia are contraindications, because digoxin makes the ventricles more irritable. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How does digoxin immune Fab reverse toxicity?","choices":["It blocks the sodium-calcium exchanger","It stimulates the sodium-potassium pump","It binds digoxin more tightly than the pump does","It restores potassium in the heart muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Its affinity for digoxin exceeds that of the sodium-potassium pump, so it pulls digoxin away and reverses toxicity rapidly. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which process underlies the ventricular arrhythmias of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Digoxin-induced afterdepolarization","Accelerated pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node","Loss of sodium-potassium ATPase from the cell","Direct calcium channel blockade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin-induced afterdepolarization makes ventricular cells fire extra beats, seen as premature ventricular beats and ventricular arrhythmia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which cardiac syndrome contraindicates digoxin?","choices":["Long QT syndrome","Brugada syndrome","Mitral valve prolapse","Wolff-Parkinson-White"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome is listed among the contraindications to digoxin. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which term names the yellow-green vision of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Xanthopsia","Nyctalopia","Diplopia","Scotoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Xanthopsia is yellow vision, with yellow-green halos and shining lights around objects, a visual sign of digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Potassium loss from a thiazide diuretic increases the toxicity of which heart failure drug?","choices":["Digoxin","Carvedilol","Spironolactone","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides increase digitalis toxicity through potassium loss, so potassium must be kept above 4.0 milliequivalents per liter. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which neurohormonal system does digoxin suppress in heart failure?","choices":["Parasympathetic nervous system","Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Baroreflex sensing system","Natriuretic peptide system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin lowers renin-angiotensin-aldosterone activity, which reduces remodeling and structural change and improves tissue perfusion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which structural features of digoxin are essential for its activity?","choices":["A lactone ring and a steroid nucleus","A lactone ring and the sugar molecules","A steroid nucleus and the sugar molecules","The sugar molecules and a benzene ring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lactone ring and the steroid nucleus are essential for activity; the attached sugar molecules only modify absorption, half-life and metabolism. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A patient taking digoxin is started on furosemide. Which concern does the combination raise?","choices":["Loss of the digoxin inotropic effect","Arrhythmias from electrolyte loss","Hyperkalemia with conduction block","Faster renal clearance of digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics waste potassium and magnesium, and low levels of both with digitalis produce arrhythmias. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
